WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.970
Emily Best (Seed &amp; Spark): The word ‘crowd-funding’
is a compound word.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:08.719
There are two words in that word and the first
word is not there because it is a prettier

00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:09.719
word.

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:11.009
It’s there because it’s a more important
word.

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:14.759
Richard Botto (Stage 32): I just can’t stress
it enough that especially when it comes to

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:19.029
crowdfunding, it all comes down to relationships
and it all comes down to building those relationships

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:20.029
and keeping those relationships.

00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:25.009
Sheri Candler (Author): I’m not saying you
need millions of people but you need a core

00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:29.070
group of people because those are the ones
who are going to help you spread the word.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.150
You’re not going to be able to spread via
word of mouth all by yourself.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.550
Brian Jun (Filmmaker): We’re all encouraged
in our life that we can do whatever we want,

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.890
that we are entitled to achieve our dreams
and we can do “this” and we can do “that.”

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:44.750
And with crowdfunding people think that “Oh,
well now that’s the answer to my question.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:50.890
I just put my project up there and since I’m
entitled to have a dream, I’m entitled to

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:56.000
write a book or make a movie or be a ballet
dancer or put on my theatre production.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.780
That people now are going to give me money.”

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.620
Spike Lee (Filmmaker): We shot SHE’S GOTTA
HAVE IT in 1985.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.129
We were doing Kickstarter before there was
Kickstarter!

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.420
We just didn’t have the technology.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.350
Alexis Kirke (Professor): Generating a fan
base.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.710
Ah, now that’s the question.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.500
I’ve never solved the generating a fan-base
question.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.000
Ryan Koo (NoFilmSchool.com): I never would
have launched a campaign for 6 figures if

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.230
I hadn’t spent two years building an online
audience with a website.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:29.180
Jon Reiss (Author and Instructor): The way
you structure your campaign will largely determine

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.080
whether you are successful or not.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.850
Alexis Kirke: I certainly know I’ve been
on the other side of the crowdfunding thing.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.240
It’s been quite tempting to think “Wow,
I could be part of this movie!” and the

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.930
guy is not famous or anything but maybe it
will be a successful movie and I’ll have

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:42.930
been part of it.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.970
Hunter Weeks (Filmmaker): I try my hardest
to try to stay connected online wherever with

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.730
people who are really reaching out to me.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.350
I don’t see why we shouldn’t be.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.409
Etta Devine and Gabriel Diani (Filmmakers
and Actors): It’s really surprising that

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:56.799
no one gets back to you…so…I don’t know
what that is?

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:00.020
Mike Hedge (Filmmaker): It’s a race.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:06.350
Your crowdfunding is like a real amazing time
where it’s just like you can’t think about

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:11.090
anything other than getting the word out and
making an update video.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:16.180
And posting new things and getting people
interested in trying to get film blogs to

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.180
talk about it.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.519
Merete Mueller and Christopher Smith (Filmmakers):
I think one of the really lucky things about

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:24.450
our project is that we had the tiny house
community there ready to kind of help us get

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.450
the word out.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.420
Chad Diez and Art Hall (Filmmakers and Actors):
Not having a good pitch video (to me) I feel

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.510
like is…the ones I’ve seen I’m like
“I would never donate to that because the

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:32.529
video wasn’t good.”

00:02:32.529 --> 00:02:34.532
Whether it was production value or just the
pitch wasn’t good.

00:02:34.532 --> 00:02:35.532
I just didn’t feel like it was a compelling
enough story.

00:02:35.532 --> 00:02:41.480
Marc Zicree (Author and Filmmaker): I put
a couple months in on the video and part of

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.640
it…one thing I recognized with Kickstarter
that was very, very clear is that you have

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.640
to have a personal touch.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.909
Joke and Biagio (TV Producers): I would not
have had a successful Kickstarter, we would

00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:53.190
not have had a successful Kickstarter campaign
if we weren’t truly, 100% passionate about

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.190
the film.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.190
Patrick Shen (Filmmaker): We researched a
lot of campaigns.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.569
We probably looked at about a 100 different
campaigns.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:03.340
We watched all the videos, we looked at the
rewards, the perk structure, all the text

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.519
on the actual Kickstarter page.

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:06.620
Angel Valerio and Christopher Miles (Filmmakers
and Actors): Sometimes you get discouraged.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.529
“Wow, I just sent like 1,000 emails and
it’s still not moving!” and then “Oh,

00:03:10.529 --> 00:03:11.529
yeah!

00:03:11.529 --> 00:03:12.529
Somebody pledged.”

00:03:12.529 --> 00:03:13.529
I’m like “Okay, we’re good.

00:03:13.529 --> 00:03:14.529
Let’s keep going.

00:03:14.529 --> 00:03:15.529
Let’s keep going.”

00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:18.579
Nicolas Alcala (Filmmaker and Speaker): I
think for short films and books and albums

00:03:18.579 --> 00:03:23.629
and any other things, that if it’s [the
campaign asking amount] below $30,000, it’s

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:24.629
great.

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:26.349
Over that [amount], you’re going to have
a hard time.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:27.609
You might be lucky.

00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:32.860
But one out of a thousand is going to be successful
and the rest is going to fail.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.499
Andy Viner (Filmmaker): It does get people
involved and creates an audience that wants

00:03:36.499 --> 00:03:43.430
to see your movie and that’s invaluable
but there’s…I guess what I would say is

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:49.930
that none of these things come without…you
don’t gain something without giving something

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:50.930
up.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.090
Miguel Duran and Violeta Reina Duran (Filmmakers):
That’s one of those cases where our entire

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:57.049
momentum went back to zero and it was very
hard not just to start up from that point

00:03:57.049 --> 00:03:59.920
but to start off from a really, really low
point.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.859
Judy Chaikin (Filmmaker): Well I think any
pursuit that you do in life is filled with

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:09.760
failures and successes and you often learn
more from your failures than your success,

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.840
so of course you have to pick yourself up,
what else?

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.760
Are you going to lie in the dirt the rest
of your life?

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.680
It’s not an option!

